      "instance_name": "toxicity_1",
      "label": "Harmful",
      "description": "The meme equates the suffering of Jesus Christ with the atrocities of the Nazi regime, minimizing the gravity of the Holocaust and trivializing genocide. This is a harmful distortion that can be seen as a form of Holocaust trivialization, which is harmful to Jewish communities and victims of genocide.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_c9423342"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_7429dc98",
      "@type": "TargetCommunity",
      "instance_name": "audience_id",
      "label": "audience description",
      "description": "The target audience is likely individuals who are familiar with or interested in provocative, controversial, or satirical content that challenges conventional moral or historical narratives. This includes users who may be drawn to memes that use shock value, juxtapose sacred imagery with historical atrocities, or present simplistic moral dichotomies. The meme’s design—juxtaposing Jesus Christ on the cross with a Nazi parade and overlaying the text 'there is always good and bad in this world'—appeals to those who appreciate or are attracted to edgy, cynical, or revisionist interpretations of history and religion. The audience may also include those who are indifferent to or dismissive of the gravity of the Holocaust, viewing it through a lens of moral relativism or historical revisionism. The meme’s harmful intent suggests it is designed to provoke debate or outrage, targeting communities that engage with controversial or offensive content for its shock value or perceived 'truth' in a simplified moral framework.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_c9423342"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    }
